WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.642
A network can find an edge anywhere in a
photo:

00:00:03.662 --> 00:00:06.518
along the face, the camera, the tripod.

00:00:06.538 --> 00:00:08.380
Does it learn a separate edge detector

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.355
for each of the sixty five thousand positions?

00:00:11.375 --> 00:00:12.030
No.

00:00:12.050 --> 00:00:15.600
It learns one small detector, and slides it
everywhere.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:18.042
Zoom in where hair meets sky.

00:00:18.062 --> 00:00:22.830
Each cell is one pixel, from zero for black to
two fifty five for white.

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:26.480
The detector is a three by three grid of
weights: a kernel.

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:29.555
Its weights are negative on the left and
positive on the right,

00:00:29.575 --> 00:00:31.625
so it compares right with left.

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:33.255
Place it over the edge.

00:00:33.275 --> 00:00:37.630
Multiply each pixel by the weight on top of
it: nine products.

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:40.530
Now add them up: six hundred seventeen.

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:42.780
One number for this position, and a big one,

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:45.275
because the patch gets brighter to the right.

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:48.687
Now shift the window one pixel, and repeat.

00:00:48.707 --> 00:00:52.820
Each position fills one cell of a new image,
called a feature map.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:53.605
Quick check.

00:00:53.625 --> 00:00:55.842
What will the kernel output on the flat sky?

00:00:55.862 --> 00:00:57.500
Pause and decide.

00:01:00.315 --> 00:01:00.870
Zero.

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:04.270
The left and right columns match, so the
products cancel.

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:07.265
Flat regions vanish, and only edges light up.

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:12.028
Run it over the whole photo, and the feature
map draws every vertical edge:

00:01:12.048 --> 00:01:15.835
orange where brightness rises to the right,
blue where it falls.

00:01:16.225 --> 00:01:21.118
And notice what never changed: the same nine
numbers, at every position.

00:01:21.138 --> 00:01:24.105
A dense layer linking every pixel to every
output

00:01:24.125 --> 00:01:27.655
would need over four billion weights for this
small image.

00:01:27.675 --> 00:01:29.650
The convolution needs nine.

00:01:30.105 --> 00:01:34.198
Change the nine numbers, and the same photo
becomes a different map.

00:01:34.218 --> 00:01:35.722
Equal weights blur it.

00:01:35.742 --> 00:01:38.385
Turn the kernel to find horizontal edges.

00:01:38.405 --> 00:01:40.698
Combine the two for every edge.

00:01:40.718 --> 00:01:45.905
A convolutional layer runs dozens of kernels
at once, and gives dozens of maps.

00:01:46.535 --> 00:01:49.803
Stack layers, and each output sees a wider
patch:

00:01:49.822 --> 00:01:52.815
two three by three layers see five by five.

00:01:52.835 --> 00:01:56.253
And like deep learning libraries, we never
flipped the kernel.

00:01:56.273 --> 00:02:01.910
Textbook convolution flips it first, so
strictly, ours is cross correlation.

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:04.148
So remember three things.

00:02:04.168 --> 00:02:06.935
A convolution is a local weighted sum.

00:02:06.955 --> 00:02:09.060
The same weights are used everywhere.

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:11.455
And different kernels see different structure.

